;
RCP/M GLOSSARY

A - Terms and abbreviations
B - Acknowledgements
C - Further reading

:A
TERMS AND ABBREVIATIONS

Term - Meaning
  ANSI / VT100 - Terminal display conventions used by the supplied
  screen-oriented programs
  Archive - A collection of preserved files or software volumes
  Bulletin board / BBS - A system for exchanging messages, and often files,
  among callers
  BYE - Resident remote-operation software; also the command to log off
  Caller - Someone using a remote system
  COM file - CP/M executable program, with a .COM extension
  CP/M - Control Program for Microcomputers, the operating system used here
  CPMUG - CP/M User Group; also the name of its software collection
  DU - Drive/user combination, for example J3:
  F4 - CP/M file attribute used by this distribution to mark caller-approved
  executables
  Forum - A bulletin board message area devoted to a subject
  LBR - CP/M library file containing other files
  Library volume - A disk or disk image containing a software collection
  Logoff - Ending a call
  Message base - The collection of stored bulletin board messages
  Named directory - A Z-System name associated with a particular DU
  Private mail - Messages addressed to a particular registered board user
  Raw TCP - Network connection carrying terminal data without Telnet
  negotiation
  RBBS - The bulletin board software supplied with the system
  RCP/M - Remote CP/M
  Resident program - Software which remains in memory while other programs
  operate
  SIG/M - A CP/M software-sharing user-group collection
  Sysop - System operator
  Terminal - The program or device used to communicate with the remote
  computer
  Transient program - A program loaded to perform a task and then return
  control to the command environment
  User area - One of CP/M's numbered file areas on a disk
  Warm boot - Return to the CP/M command environment without a full machine
  restart
  WORK - Temporary caller workspace in I0:
  XMODEM / YMODEM - File transfer protocols supported by the supplied
  upload/download services
  Z-System - The enhanced CP/M command environment from which Z3PLUS developed

:B
ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS

HUMONGOUS RCP/M owes its existence to the work of a great many people, some of
whom made their contributions more than forty years ago. Much of our work has
been to bring their software together, adapt it to present-day computers and
make it available to another generation of CP/M enthusiasts.

Our particular thanks go to Gary Kildall and the team at Digital Research for
CP/M; Udo Munk for z80pack and cpmsim; Richard Conn for his pioneering work on
ZCPR; and Jay Sage and Bridger Mitchell for Z3PLUS, with Joe Wright for his
contributions to the Z-System.

We also owe a debt to Dave Jaffe, the original author of BYE, and to Wayne
Masters, Irv Hoff, George Peace and the other contributors who carried its
development forward; to the authors and maintainers of CP/M RBBS, whose work
gives our callers a place to exchange messages; and to Jim Brain for tcpser,
which allows us to recreate the modem connection without a telephone line.

Finally, our thanks to the many authors of CP/M utilities, the SIG/M and CPMUG
librarians and contributors, and the archivists and volunteers who have kept
this remarkable body of software and documentation accessible. Without their
generosity and persistence, there would be very little for an RCP/M such as
ours to offer.

The individual software distributions retain their original credits and
notices.

:C
FURTHER READING

If you decide you'd like to establish a board of your own, the companion
Sysop's Guide describes the installation, configuration and maintenance of
HUMONGOUS RCP/M.

For a glimpse of what calling an RCP/M was like in its heyday, see Jon
Schneider's 1985 guide and other period material in the historical RCP/M
archive. The command set and restrictions of those systems may differ from
ours.
